ully backed up  by much after she appeared on the bbc  program  what happened before the big bang um  and is going to definitely have some  really fascinating debates i suspect  with martin  on some of these points our third  speaker is hilary  lawson he is a postmodern philosopher  and a renowned critic of philosophical  realism  he's the author of reflexivity the  post-modern predicament  which argues that the paradoxes of  self-reference  have been central to 20th century  philosophy he is perhaps best known for  his  theory of closure which puts forward  non-realist metaphysics  and contends that we close the openness  of the world  with our thought and language last but  not least  is kate dublin she is a computer  scientist  sex robot theorist senior lecturer in  social and cultural artificial  intelligence at  king's college london and the author of  turned on  science sex and robots she has  previously said that instead of fearing  the rise of machines  we could quite literally embrace them  so introduction's over we're going to  dive in  each speaker is going to address a  question and give three minutes  to that question and then we'll proceed  on the debate  so first just three minutes times four  speakers is 12.  the question that we'll go for first is  should we give up talk of artificial  intelligence in terms of  super intelligence for instance and  focus on more limited  applications such as automation and  machine learning  so martin reese your thoughts in three  minutes  please yes well i think we should avoid  semantics  and just ask what machines will do and  what they're unlikely ever to do  the main point is that they work a  million times or more faster than us  so they can absorb data and process data  much quicker  and that's why they can do amazing  things they do amazing calculations  they can uh monitor and control  elaborate networks um and they're very  useful for that  um and they can um learn as we  know to play games like s and go  and do better than human beings but  essentially because they can play  against themselves one game per second  and so they gain experience very very  fast  and of course um what we worry about  is if decisions about us are made by a  machine  then do we trust it even if it's  normally right  are you prepared to uh accept its  judgments uh if  it's going to decide whether we uh uh  get released in prison  have an operation or get credit  something like that that's a big issue  but  i think we must be excited about the  prospects of  um advanced artificial intelligence um  certainly it can help  a quants stock broker to do better than  any human player  if the chinese wanted to have a  completely planned economy  they could have it in a way that mark  could never dream of by being able to uh  gather all the data and process it and  uh  in science um i think it will  enable us to make discoveries because it  can already do many things better than  humans  can human mathematicians you can analyze  data much much faster  find weak correlations we would miss and  uh to touch base a bit with lara  i think if the uh basic physics of the  big bang  involves string theory in 10 or 11  dimensions the mathematics may be too  hard for any human  but ai may do the calculations and spew  out the  other and if that answer accords with  observation  then we have to take the theory  seriously so there's huge potential  but of course the science fiction  idea is that these machines will  interact with the uh the real world  and here it's much more difficult  because they can only interact with the  external world  via something like the internet of  things um and we know that robots are  very clumsy  compared to real people and so when  people talk about the kind of jobs that  are going to be taken over  by uh machines um it's more likely to be  certain  um white collar jobs um accountancy and  things like that  i don't imagine ever that a machine is  going to be able to be a plumber 